.\" -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-
.\" mpmtr-app-http(1). Keep in sync with cmd/mpmtr-app-http/main.go.
.TH MPMTR\-APP\-HTTP 1 "November 2026" "mpmtr" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mpmtr\-app\-http \- application-check plugin for mpmtr: does the server answer HTTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mpmtr \-\-app
.RB \(dq http
.RB [ \-\-tls | \-\-plain ]
.RB [ \-\-verify ]
.RB [ \-\-get
.RI [ URL ]]
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-user\-agent
.IR TEXT ]\(dq
.RI [ options ]
.IR target [: port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mpmtr\-app\-http
is a plugin of
.BR mpmtr (1).
It is not run by hand:
.B mpmtr \-\-app http
starts it and hands it connections.
Its own options go into the quotes after its name.
.PP
.B mpmtr
measures with bare TCP SYN packets, which tell only that a server accepts
connections.
With this plugin every measured source port also gets, every
.B \-\-app\-interval
(default 10 s), one real HTTP request to the server that port leads to.
A server that accepts connections and does not answer HTTP is then seen as
such: its checks fail while TCP answers, and after three failed checks in a
row it is DOWN.
.SH WHAT ONE CHECK DOES
.IP 1. 4
.B mpmtr
opens a kernel TCP connection from the measured source port to the target:
the same addresses and ports its probes use, so a load balancer leads the
connection to the same server.
.IP 2. 4
The plugin gets the connected socket. On port 443 (or with
.BR \-\-tls )
it first makes a TLS handshake, with the target's name as the server name.
Then it sends
.RS 4
.PP
.EX
HEAD / HTTP/1.0
Host: \fIthe target as given to mpmtr, without the port\fP
User\-Agent: mpmtr
.EE
.RE
.IP 3. 4
It reads one line of the answer.
A line that starts with
.B HTTP/
is a passed check, whatever the status code.
.IP 4. 4
.B mpmtr
closes the connection with RST.
.PP
The whole check must fit in
.B \-\-app\-timeout
(default 5 s).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-tls
Speak TLS. Without it TLS is spoken on port 443 only.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Never speak TLS, also on port 443.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
A certificate that does not verify against the name fails the check (stage
.BR tls ).
By default the certificate is not verified: the check asks whether the
server answers, not whether its certificate is in order.
.TP
.BR \-\-get " [\fIURL\fP]"
Send
.B GET
in place of
.BR HEAD .
Some servers refuse HEAD or answer it differently (a 418 or 405 where GET
gets 200). Only the status line is read either way.
.I URL
says what to ask for in place of
.BR / :
a path
.RB ( /index.php ),
or a URL with a host
.RB ( example.com/index.php ,
.BR https://example.com/index.php ).
The host of the URL is then the name asked for (the
.B Host
header and the TLS server name), and its scheme says whether TLS is spoken;
.BR \-\-host ", " \-\-tls " and " \-\-plain
win over them.
The connection still goes to the target given to
.BR mpmtr .
.TP
.BI \-\-host " NAME"
The name for the
.B Host
header and the TLS server name, in place of the target given to
.BR mpmtr .
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-agent " TEXT"
The
.B User\-Agent
header (default
.BR mpmtr ).
Some sites answer a client they do not take for a browser differently, for
example with a redirect to another version of the site.
.SH RESULTS
.TP
passed
The server sent an HTTP status line. Its status code is shown in the
.B PLUGIN
column of the server's row.
Counts as an answer of that port and server; its RTT is the time of the TCP
handshake of this connection.
.TP
.RB "failed, stage " tls
The TLS handshake failed (or, with
.BR \-\-verify ,
the certificate did not verify).
.TP
.RB "failed, stage " send
The request could not be written (the server reset the connection).
.TP
.RB "failed, stage " answer
No line came before the timeout, the server closed the connection without
one, or the line is not an HTTP status line.
.PP
The checks are counted apart from the TCP probes: a failed one is shown as
.BI app\-failed: N / M
in the flags and as
.BR app_failed " of " app_checks
in JSON, with a rate of its own, and is not part of the TCP failure share.
Three failed checks in a row make the port DOWN, reason
.BR app_down .
A connection that was refused or never established is counted by
.B mpmtr
itself, before the plugin sees anything.
.PP
To see every check with its stage and error text, set
.B MPMTR_DEBUG_APP=1
and read standard error.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT CHECK
.IP \(bu 2
The status code.
404, 500 and a redirect all pass: the check answers "is an HTTP server
alive here", not "does the site work".
.IP \(bu 2
The certificate, unless
.B \-\-verify
is given.
.IP \(bu 2
More than one site.
Every check asks for the target's own name; other sites of the same server
are not asked.
.PP
A check of your own protocol is a small program: see
.B docs/WRITING_PLUGINS.md
in the source tree.
.SH EXAMPLES
Every route to a web server, each measured port checked over HTTP:
.RS
.EX
mpmtr \-\-app http example.com:80
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every server behind an anycast address, checked every 30 s:
.RS
.EX
mpmtr \-\-anycast \-\-app http \-\-app\-interval 30s example.com:80
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.B mpmtr
looks for the executable
.B mpmtr\-app\-http
next to itself, then in
.BR PATH .
.SH SECURITY
The plugin runs with the privileges
.B mpmtr
has.
It sends nothing but the request above, and only over connections
.B mpmtr
gave it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mpmtr (1),
.BR mpmtr\-hop\-snmp (1)
.PP
.B docs/PLUGINS.md
(the plugin protocol),
.B docs/WRITING_PLUGINS.md
(how to write a plugin).
